  <div class="chart-wrapper">
    <div class="chart-title">252-Day Rolling Annualized Volatility (%)</div>
    <div class="chart-subtitle">Standard deviation of daily returns × √252, expressed as percentage</div>
    <div class="chart-container" style="height:280px">
      <canvas id="volChart"></canvas>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="insight-grid">
    <div class="insight-card">
      <div class="insight-icon">📉</div>
      <div class="insight-title">GFC Volatility Spike (2008–2009)</div>
      <div class="insight-body">The Global Financial Crisis drove rolling 1-year volatility above <strong>40%+</strong>, as NKE fell ~40% from its pre-crisis highs. Consumer staples linkage provided partial insulation relative to financials.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="insight-card">
      <div class="insight-icon">🦠</div>
      <div class="insight-title">COVID-19 Shock (2020)</div>
      <div class="insight-body">March 2020 saw an acute volatility burst with intraday ranges expanding sharply. However, NKE recovered within months, benefiting from accelerated DTC adoption and athleisure tailwinds.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="insight-card">
      <div class="insight-icon">⚠️</div>
      <div class="insight-title">Post-ATH Regime (2022–2025)</div>
      <div class="insight-body">Unlike prior bear phases, the 2022–2025 drawdown was <strong>sustained and structural</strong> — elevated volatility persisted across multiple years as NKE re-rated significantly lower from its $166 peak.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="insight-card">
      <div class="insight-icon">📊</div>
      <div class="insight-title">Full-Period Annualized Vol: {ann_vol:.1f}%</div>
      <div class="insight-body">Over the complete 26-year horizon, NKE's annualized volatility of <strong>{ann_vol:.1f}%</strong> is consistent with large-cap consumer discretionary — notably higher than staples but below small-cap or tech benchmarks.</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
</section>

<!-- VOLUME -->
<section class="section">
<div class="container">
  <div class="section-label">04 · Trading Volume Patterns</div>
  <div class="section-title">Volume Trends & Price-Volume Correlation</div>
  <div class="section-desc">Average monthly volume contextualized against price action. Volume spikes typically coincide with earnings reactions, macro dislocations, or structural re-ratings — not routine trading days.</div>

  <div class="chart-wrapper">
    <div class="chart-title">Average Monthly Trading Volume (Millions of Shares)</div>
    <div class="chart-subtitle">Monthly mean of daily share volume · Notable spikes annotated</div>
    <div class="chart-container" style="height:280px">
      <canvas id="volChart2"></canvas>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="insight-grid">
    <div class="insight-card">
      <div class="insight-icon">📈</div>
      <div class="insight-title">Highest Single-Day Volume</div>
      <div class="insight-body"><strong>Jun 28, 2024: ~130M shares</strong> traded — the highest single day in the dataset. NKE fell ~20% that session following a severe earnings miss and guidance cut, triggering institutional repositioning.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="insight-card">
      <div class="insight-icon">🔻</div>
      <div class="insight-title">Volume Decline (2013–2022)</div>
      <div class="insight-body">Average daily volume fell from ~14–17M shares in 2006–2008 to <strong>6–8M shares in 2018–2022</strong>, partly reflecting index weight dilution, algorithmic fragmentation, and reduced retail activity during a steady bull run.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="insight-card">
      <div class="insight-icon">🌊</div>
      <div class="insight-title">2024–2025 Volume Resurgence</div>
      <div class="insight-body">Average daily volume surged back to <strong>11–17M shares</strong> in 2024–2025, consistent with a major re-rating event — elevated two-way flow as the stock declined from ~$100 toward $55, attracting both sellers and value seekers.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="insight-card">
      <div class="insight-icon">🔁</div>
      <div class="insight-title">Volume as Confirmation Signal</div>
      <div class="insight-body">The Feb 2000 sell-off, Sep 2001 turbulence, and the 2008 crisis all saw volume spike alongside price declines — a classic distribution pattern. Notably, the 2009–2020 bull run was <strong>low-volume and steady</strong>, suggesting institutional accumulation.</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
</section>

